!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/schema-sidecar.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/sidecar-common.tn"
@doc:"""
  Formal shape of a schema-layer sidecar (tests/class2/schema/**/*-expected.tn) -- [TSON-SCHEMA]
  §5's declaration forms and §8's resolved output. See README.md's "The Class 2 layers" and
  RUNNER.md.

  This is the first layer of Class 2, and the one that needs no invented expectation format. §1.3
  makes producing a resolved schema value a MUST and §8 fixes its serialization, so a vector's
  subject is a schema document and its expected side is the resolved output that document denotes,
  written in the spec's own §8 form.
  """
!schema {
  @doc:"""
    A resolved schema, as §8.1's ingest form: a `!schema` map from each name to the value its entry
    resolves to -- `name => !record { ... }`, a type body carrying its own `chain` --
    exactly what the spec's resolver-output fixtures (`*-resolved.tn`) carry and what §8.2 keys
    identity on.

    Carried as a multi-line token rather than modelled field by field, which would restate meta.tn
    inside this schema and leave two statements of one shape to drift apart. A runner reads the text
    with its own reader for meta.tn's `meta.schema` -- so the payload is validated against the spec's own
    schema, by the implementation under test, rather than against a paraphrase of it here. Nothing
    is escaped on the way: a multi-line token is raw text, which is why this stays inside the
    conservative sidecar subset RUNNER.md rule 2 states.

    The `subtypes` index §8.2 calls resolver-managed is part of that output and is stated where a vector
    has one: the layer is about what a schema document resolves to, and an entry that another entry
    composes with says so here. What §2.2.3's import closure makes of a *namespace* is the link layer's,
    which is why nothing at this layer states an imported name.

    A template's resolved output is an ordinary record: an open record, a `!meta.record` carrying
    `parameters` (§5.10), each parameter written where it stands as its name, a reference value looked up
    in the record's `parameters` before the namespace. Read back, it binds the value the resolver built,
    so the two sides of a vector compare as values like any other entry's. An application closes to a
    `!meta.record` without `parameters` and states its application on its key as `@meta.application`; a
    construction states its constructor and carries none.

    The resolved document carries no header. Its names are read under the subject's own imports --
    under the pre-loaded kernel alone when the subject has none -- so an instance of a constructor a
    fixture schema declares is read against that fixture, and a runner supplies the imports when it
    reads.
    """
  schema_valid => {
    resolved: text
  }

  @doc:"""
    An `error` vector's category is `resolver` throughout this layer. [TSON-DATA] §8.1 says so
    outright for Part 2: "every error that makes a schema fail to load or ingest -- incoherent
    constraint values, invalid defaults, refuted assertions, failed ingest checks -- is a resolver
    error, however value-like the violated rule, because it is detected while resolving the schema.
    Validation errors are reserved for data checked against a successfully loaded schema."

    So a bound that cannot be met and a default of the wrong type are both `resolver` here, and
    `validation` at this layer would be wrong however much the rule reads like a constraint.
    """
  @doc:"""
    `refused` is [TSON-DATA] §8.1's fifth outcome at this layer, and the schema-side peer of the reader
    layer's. §8.2's name-hygiene mechanisms refuse a schema without making it invalid, and [TSON-SCHEMA]
    §11.4 supplies the scopes they run over -- the members of one enum, the field names of one record
    definition with its groups' member labels, the declared names of one schema, and the merged namespace
    at `!!import`.

    It is a distinct outcome from `error` for the reason §11.4 gives: the mechanisms read data the Unicode
    Consortium declines to freeze, and as validity rules they would make an import of two independently
    published schemas impossible to write where a skeleton happens to collide. A refused schema is one this
    processor declines, under a policy a deployment may relax in code -- not one the format says is wrong.
    """
  schema_sidecar => sidecar_common & {
    ( valid: schema_valid | error: vector_error | refused: vector_refusal )
  }
}
